Increase r8152's USB receive buffers (RTL8153 on USB 2.0).

With 10 buffers of 16 KB, the LG lm21u TV (MStar EHCI) does not drain the adapter's
internal queue fast enough: packets are lost before they reach the kernel (~17,700
out-of-order TCP segments per 300 MB) and receiving stays at ~152 Mbit/s on the LAN.
With 32 buffers of 32 KB (the same receive buffer size mainline Linux's r8152 uses
for the RTL8153), receiving goes to ~215 Mbit/s and out-of-order segments drop to
~600. The size is passed to the chip by r8153_set_rx_early_size().

Measured on 2026-11-05 on an OLED55A1PSA (webOS 6.5.3), PC -> TV over HTTP, 300 MB:
  original (10 x 16 KB)  152-153 Mbit/s
  32 x 16 KB             155-159 Mbit/s
  10 x 32 KB             202-212 Mbit/s
  32 x 32 KB             214-215 Mbit/s   <- this patch

--- a/r8152.c
+++ b/r8152.c
@@ -463,7 +463,7 @@
 };
 
 #define RTL8152_MAX_TX		4
-#define RTL8152_MAX_RX		10
+#define RTL8152_MAX_RX		32
 #define INTBUFSIZE		2
 #define CRC_SIZE		4
 #define TX_ALIGN		4
@@ -654,7 +654,7 @@
  * The RTL chips use a 64 element hash table based on the Ethernet CRC.
  */
 static const int multicast_filter_limit = 32;
-static unsigned int agg_buf_sz = 16384;
+static unsigned int agg_buf_sz = 32768;
 
 #define RTL_LIMITED_TSO_SIZE	(agg_buf_sz - sizeof(struct tx_desc) - \
 				 VLAN_ETH_HLEN - VLAN_HLEN)
